{"id":"obligations/theory/T38","statement":"For finite nonempty families of nonempty closed real intervals, pairwise nonempty intersections suffice for finite families; this is not a claim about every lattice form. Convex sets in R^d require the hypotheses of Helly and witnesses up to d+1. A three-arc discrete circle and fixed halfplanes distinguish two- and three-member empty meets.","status":"proven","references":["test/t38-forms.test.ts","vectors/laws/t38/circle12.json","vectors/laws/t38/halfplanes.json"],"proof":["Let L=max_i l_i and U=min_i u_i; finiteness and a nonempty family ensure both are attained at r,s.","If r=s, nonemptiness gives L<=U. Otherwise pairwise intersection gives l_r<=u_s, hence L<=U.","The total meet is [L,U]. If L>U the distinct extremal pair r,s itself has empty meet."]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/F2","statement":"On twelve discrete points, five-point arcs starting at 0, 4 and 8 meet in every pair and not jointly. This finite example does not prove the absence of every finite Helly bound.","status":"proven","references":["vectors/laws/t38/circle12.json","test/t38-forms.test.ts"],"proof":["The explicitly listed finite arcs have pair intersections {4}, {0}, {8}; their triple intersection is empty."]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/F3","statement":"The four seller/buyer/carrier/auditor halfplanes conflict with seller/auditor witness; x>=0,y>=0,x+y<=-1 is pairwise consistent and jointly empty. Fixed nonnegative certificates verify these finite cases only.","status":"proven","references":["vectors/laws/t38/halfplanes.json","test/t38-forms.test.ts"],"proof":["Nonnegative weights (1,0,0,1) combine the four inequalities into 0<=-2, certifying the seller/auditor conflict.","Weights (1,1,1) combine x>=0, y>=0, x+y<=-1 into 0<=-1. The supplied witness for each pair proves that this three-member conflict is minimal."]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/F4","statement":"The handed expression Q(n,p,s)=log2(n/p)-h(s/p) has proved integer-domain extrema under p|n and 0<=s<p. Its identification with Shannon information or a physical worst-case phase objective is not established: n=p=2,s=1 gives Q=-1.","status":"open","references":["vectors/laws/t87/phase-controls.json","samples/laws/t87/phase-domain.json","test/measures/t87-phase.test.ts"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/T87","statement":"For two unit-amplitude phases a,b, squared resultant intensity is 2+2*cos(a-b). With principal separation delta=acos(cos(a-b)), intensity is below two iff delta>pi/2. This identity proves no global laminarity or policy advantage.","status":"proven","references":["test/measures/t87-phase.test.ts","test/statements-consumers-042.test.ts"],"proof":["Expand (cos(a)+cos(b))^2+(sin(a)+sin(b))^2 and apply cos(a-b)=cos(a)cos(b)+sin(a)sin(b).","On delta in [0,pi], cos(delta)<0 iff delta>pi/2."]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/F1","statement":"For all partitions X,Y of a finite n-element set with uniform measure, RI<=c_n=log2(n)-2+2/n. For n>=3 equality holds exactly for the alternating path configuration and its relabellings (including exchange of X,Y); for n=1,2 every pair has RI=0. Independent-origin verification is a separate open provenance demand.","status":"proven","references":["vectors/laws/f1/path-witness.json","test/f1-boundary-042.test.ts","test/laws-papers.test.ts"],"proof":["F1/graph-identity: If meet is trivial, the bipartite block graph is connected. One vertex per X/Y block; a nonempty intersection gives one edge of integer weight c, vertex degree a is total incident weight, and total edge weight is n. With phi(t)=t log2(t), H(X)=log2(n)-sum_X phi(a)/n, H(Y) analogously, H(join)=log2(n)-sum_edges phi(c)/n, H(meet)=0. Hence RI=log2(n)-Phi/n with Phi=sum_vertices phi(a)-sum_edges phi(c).","F1/overlap-split: For an edge with weight c>=2, choose one incident block A with size a>=c, pull one atom in that intersection out of A into a new singleton block, and leave the other partition unchanged. The original intersection retains c-1 atoms, so no original graph vertex or connection disappears; the new singleton is a leaf, preserving connectivity and trivial meet. Phi_old-Phi_new=[phi(a)-phi(a-1)]-[phi(c)-phi(c-1)]>=0: phi is strictly convex on positive reals (phi_second(t)=1/(t ln(2))). Thus RI_new>=RI_old, strictly if a>c. The integer potential sum_edges(c-1)=n-edge_count decreases by one; finitely many splits reach discrete join.","F1/strict-overlap: When n>=3 and some edge has c>=2, if either endpoint has size greater than c, choose it for a strict improvement. Otherwise both endpoint sizes equal c; connectedness then forces this to be the sole edge and n=c. The first split may preserve Phi. Since n>=3, the remaining edge has weight n-1>=2; split its opposite endpoint, whose size is still n>n-1, to obtain a strict improvement. Therefore any weighted-overlap pair is strictly below the maximum for n>=3.","F1/degree-floor: For integer d>=1, phi(d)>=2(d-1), equality exactly at d=1,2. Equality is direct at 1,2. For real d>=2, the derivative of phi(d)-2(d-1) is log2(d)+1/ln(2)-2>0.","F1/simple-graph-upper-equality: At discrete join every edge weight is one. The connected simple bipartite graph has n edges, v<=n+1 vertices and sum_degrees=2n. Thus Phi=sum phi(d)>=2(2n-v)>=2(n-1), giving RI<=log2(n)-2+2/n=c_n. Equality requires v=n+1 and all degrees in {1,2}: a connected tree with maximum degree two is a path. Conversely a path has two degree-one vertices and n-1 degree-two vertices and attains c_n.","F1/trivial-meet-upper-equality: A trivial-meet weighted pair can be split to a simple connected pair without decreasing RI; the latter is bounded by c_n. For n>=3 strict-overlap excludes weighted pairs from equality, leaving exactly simple paths, including their atom relabellings and interchange of X/Y.","F1/component-decomposition: For a nontrivial meet, its blocks are the connected components C_t of the block graph, with sizes n_t. X, Y and join refine meet, so entropy chain rules give H(X)=H(meet)+sum_t(n_t/n)H(X|C_t), and likewise for Y and join. Consequently RI=sum_t(n_t/n)RI_t, each restricted pair having trivial meet.","F1/component-upper-equality: Every RI_t<=c_(n_t). Here c_1=c_2=0; for real x>=2, c_prime(x)=1/(x ln(2))-2/x^2>0. Therefore c_k<c_n for every integer 1<=k<n when n>=3. With more than one component, all n_t<n, and RI<=sum_t(n_t/n)c_(n_t)<c_n. Thus equality for n>=3 forces trivial meet and the path characterization above.","F1/small-boundary: For n=1 the unique partition pair gives RI=0=c_1. For n=2 the two partitions form a chain; for any pair its meet and join are the pair itself in some order, so RI=0=c_2. Hence every pair attains the zero maximum in these two cases."]}
{"id":"theory/source/L1","statement":"Distinctly named fixed claims accumulate by set union in the knowledge order; this is different from union of admissible values. Bounds on one cell are read by their form meet. Arrival permutation does not erase or replace the effective order of signatures and witnessed widenings.","status":"proven","references":["test/knowledge-laws-042.test.ts","vectors/laws/knowledge-laws.json"],"proof":["Set union is associative, commutative and idempotent. The two named claims remain present in either arrival order, while the held value is their interval meet.","Reordering stored marks with distinct effective times preserves the same read order; changing effective times or handed tie order can change the value."]}
{"id":"theory/source/L2","statement":"Finite families of convex sets in R^d satisfy Helly with checks on every subfamily of at most d+1 members. Intervals give Helly 2. Circular discrete arcs have no uniform Helly bound across sizes, though a fixed n-point circle has sharp bound n. Noncrossing arcs with a fixed cut reduce to intervals.","status":"proven","references":["test/t38-forms.test.ts","vectors/laws/t38/halfplanes.json","vectors/laws/t38/circle12.json"],"proof":["For d+2 points in R^d, affine dependence supplies coefficients alpha_i with sum alpha_i=0 and sum alpha_i*x_i=0. Positive and negative coefficients have equal positive total, yielding a common convex-combination point z (Radon).","For d+2 convex sets, choose x_i in all sets except C_i. For each C_i, one Radon group omits i; all its points belong to C_i, so z belongs to C_i by convexity.","For a larger finite family, replace the final two sets by their intersection. Every subfamily of at most d+1 of the reduced family intersects: those involving the merged set reduce to the d+2 base case. Induction gives the whole intersection.","The d coordinate inequalities x_i>=0 and sum_i x_i<=-1 are jointly inconsistent but every proper subfamily is feasible; thus d+1 is sharp for d>=1.","For circular arcs A_i=Z_n minus {i}, the meet indexed by J equals Z_n minus J; only the complete n-member family is empty. Choosing n larger than any proposed bound refutes a uniform bound."]}
{"id":"theory/source/L3","statement":"If either preserved incompatible candidate can be the truth and the supplied evidence cannot distinguish them, selecting one can exclude truth in an admissible assignment. Arrival order cannot justify that selection; the existing object retains both conflicting claims.","status":"proven","references":["test/knowledge-laws-042.test.ts","vectors/laws/knowledge-laws.json"],"proof":["For any candidate removed by a selection, choose the admissible assignment in which that candidate is true. The supplied evidence is unchanged, so the selection provides no guarantee covering all such assignments."]}
{"id":"theory/source/L4","statement":"Under justified failure bounds p_i in one probability space, simultaneous success has probability at least max(0,1-sum_i p_i). Agreement and copy counts do not supply those bounds; an additional origin need not close any information or spend a positive probability amount.","status":"proven","references":["src/measures/confidence.ts","test/measures/confidence.test.ts"],"proof":["For failure events F_i, 1_(union F_i)<=sum_i 1_(F_i) pointwise.","Expectation gives P(union F_i)<=sum_i P(F_i)<=sum_i p_i. Complement and nonnegativity give max(0,1-sum_i p_i).","The empty family yields success probability 1; sum_i p_i>=1 yields only the trivial lower bound 0. No independence premise is used."]}
{"id":"theory/source/L5","statement":"For finite uniform partitions, residual information I(X;Y)-H(meet) is at most c_n=log2(n)-2+2/n. A path pair for n>=3 has positive mutual information despite a trivial common coarsening; a nonconstant common carrier is not implied by that information.","status":"proven","references":["vectors/laws/f1/path-witness.json","test/f1-boundary-042.test.ts"],"proof":["F1/graph-identity: If meet is trivial, the bipartite block graph is connected. One vertex per X/Y block; a nonempty intersection gives one edge of integer weight c, vertex degree a is total incident weight, and total edge weight is n. With phi(t)=t log2(t), H(X)=log2(n)-sum_X phi(a)/n, H(Y) analogously, H(join)=log2(n)-sum_edges phi(c)/n, H(meet)=0. Hence RI=log2(n)-Phi/n with Phi=sum_vertices phi(a)-sum_edges phi(c).","F1/overlap-split: For an edge with weight c>=2, choose one incident block A with size a>=c, pull one atom in that intersection out of A into a new singleton block, and leave the other partition unchanged. The original intersection retains c-1 atoms, so no original graph vertex or connection disappears; the new singleton is a leaf, preserving connectivity and trivial meet. Phi_old-Phi_new=[phi(a)-phi(a-1)]-[phi(c)-phi(c-1)]>=0: phi is strictly convex on positive reals (phi_second(t)=1/(t ln(2))). Thus RI_new>=RI_old, strictly if a>c. The integer potential sum_edges(c-1)=n-edge_count decreases by one; finitely many splits reach discrete join.","F1/strict-overlap: When n>=3 and some edge has c>=2, if either endpoint has size greater than c, choose it for a strict improvement. Otherwise both endpoint sizes equal c; connectedness then forces this to be the sole edge and n=c. The first split may preserve Phi. Since n>=3, the remaining edge has weight n-1>=2; split its opposite endpoint, whose size is still n>n-1, to obtain a strict improvement. Therefore any weighted-overlap pair is strictly below the maximum for n>=3.","F1/degree-floor: For integer d>=1, phi(d)>=2(d-1), equality exactly at d=1,2. Equality is direct at 1,2. For real d>=2, the derivative of phi(d)-2(d-1) is log2(d)+1/ln(2)-2>0.","F1/simple-graph-upper-equality: At discrete join every edge weight is one. The connected simple bipartite graph has n edges, v<=n+1 vertices and sum_degrees=2n. Thus Phi=sum phi(d)>=2(2n-v)>=2(n-1), giving RI<=log2(n)-2+2/n=c_n. Equality requires v=n+1 and all degrees in {1,2}: a connected tree with maximum degree two is a path. Conversely a path has two degree-one vertices and n-1 degree-two vertices and attains c_n.","F1/trivial-meet-upper-equality: A trivial-meet weighted pair can be split to a simple connected pair without decreasing RI; the latter is bounded by c_n. For n>=3 strict-overlap excludes weighted pairs from equality, leaving exactly simple paths, including their atom relabellings and interchange of X/Y.","F1/component-decomposition: For a nontrivial meet, its blocks are the connected components C_t of the block graph, with sizes n_t. X, Y and join refine meet, so entropy chain rules give H(X)=H(meet)+sum_t(n_t/n)H(X|C_t), and likewise for Y and join. Consequently RI=sum_t(n_t/n)RI_t, each restricted pair having trivial meet.","F1/component-upper-equality: Every RI_t<=c_(n_t). Here c_1=c_2=0; for real x>=2, c_prime(x)=1/(x ln(2))-2/x^2>0. Therefore c_k<c_n for every integer 1<=k<n when n>=3. With more than one component, all n_t<n, and RI<=sum_t(n_t/n)c_(n_t)<c_n. Thus equality for n>=3 forces trivial meet and the path characterization above.","F1/small-boundary: For n=1 the unique partition pair gives RI=0=c_1. For n=2 the two partitions form a chain; for any pair its meet and join are the pair itself in some order, so RI=0=c_2. Hence every pair attains the zero maximum in these two cases."]}
{"id":"theory/source/L6","statement":"Deterministic injective copies of one observation preserve its information about truth. The qualified covering-cause upper bound requires the declared uniform-truth, independent-indicator and symmetric active-channel conditions.","status":"proven","references":["test/measures/t12-capacity.test.ts","vectors/laws/knowledge-laws.json"],"proof":["For truth T and independent covering indicator C, I(T;Y) <= I(T;Y,C) = (1-c) I(T;Y|C=0) + c I(T;Y|C=1). The first conditional information is at most log2(n); the copied symmetric active symbol has conditional entropy h(q)+(1-q)log2(n-1), uniform marginal and information log2(n) minus that entropy. Combining terms gives the T12 bound; a deterministic injective replication of Y preserves its information. No achievability or private-channel model follows.","A tuple of one or more identical copies determines the original symbol and vice versa; the generated information is unchanged."]}
{"id":"theory/source/L7","statement":"For a symmetric covering channel and uniform truth, conditional active unanimity on v has probability 1/n; its joint active contribution is c/n, independent of q. Whole-mixture unanimity includes the inactive contribution and is not generally c/n. Unlabelled copied observations of that active channel cannot identify q.","status":"proven","references":["test/knowledge-laws-042.test.ts","vectors/laws/knowledge-laws.json"],"proof":["Under uniform truth and a symmetric active n-ary channel, P(Y=v|C=1)=(q+(n-1)*(1-q)/(n-1))/n=1/n. Copies add no new event condition. Multiplication by P(C=1)=c yields the joint active contribution, not the unconditioned whole-mixture event.","The active observed tuple is (v,...,v) with probability 1/n for every q; distinct q therefore give identical unlabelled observation distributions."]}
{"id":"theory/source/L8","statement":"The proposed dissent-only and outsider-if-and-only-if identification claims require a specified partial symmetric-cause observation model. Existing M/3 identification consumes truth-labelled population moments and handed sharing sets; it does not prove those unlabelled q claims.","status":"open","references":["src/measures/identification.ts","test/measures/identification.test.ts","vectors/laws/knowledge-laws.json"]}
{"id":"theory/source/L9","statement":"In the existing handed M/3 model, positive compatible population moments yield shared log-survival equations. Full column rank gives uniqueness for the shared parameters; the sharing matrix alone does not verify positivity, model fit or singleton/private decomposition.","status":"proven","references":["src/measures/identification.ts","test/measures/identification.test.ts","vectors/laws/knowledge-laws.json"],"proof":["For positive correctness and compatible handed independent factor survivals, log(P(correct_a,correct_b)/(P(correct_a)P(correct_b))) is the sum of negative log survivals for factors shared by a and b. Full column rank identifies those shared log survivals in this model; singleton/private causes do not supply shared columns and remain unresolved. Zero correctness or incompatible moments invalidates this linearization even with the same declared sharing matrix.","If A has full column rank, A*u=b has at most one solution. Exponentiation recovers the positive shared survivals. This claim is conditional on the existing model and does not identify singleton/private factors."]}
{"id":"theory/source/F1","statement":"For all partitions X,Y of a finite n-element set with uniform measure, RI<=c_n=log2(n)-2+2/n. For n>=3 equality holds exactly for the alternating path configuration and its relabellings (including exchange of X,Y); for n=1,2 every pair has RI=0. Independent-origin verification is a separate open provenance demand.","status":"proven","references":["vectors/laws/f1/path-witness.json","test/f1-boundary-042.test.ts","test/laws-papers.test.ts"],"proof":["F1/graph-identity: If meet is trivial, the bipartite block graph is connected. One vertex per X/Y block; a nonempty intersection gives one edge of integer weight c, vertex degree a is total incident weight, and total edge weight is n. With phi(t)=t log2(t), H(X)=log2(n)-sum_X phi(a)/n, H(Y) analogously, H(join)=log2(n)-sum_edges phi(c)/n, H(meet)=0. Hence RI=log2(n)-Phi/n with Phi=sum_vertices phi(a)-sum_edges phi(c).","F1/overlap-split: For an edge with weight c>=2, choose one incident block A with size a>=c, pull one atom in that intersection out of A into a new singleton block, and leave the other partition unchanged. The original intersection retains c-1 atoms, so no original graph vertex or connection disappears; the new singleton is a leaf, preserving connectivity and trivial meet. Phi_old-Phi_new=[phi(a)-phi(a-1)]-[phi(c)-phi(c-1)]>=0: phi is strictly convex on positive reals (phi_second(t)=1/(t ln(2))). Thus RI_new>=RI_old, strictly if a>c. The integer potential sum_edges(c-1)=n-edge_count decreases by one; finitely many splits reach discrete join.","F1/strict-overlap: When n>=3 and some edge has c>=2, if either endpoint has size greater than c, choose it for a strict improvement. Otherwise both endpoint sizes equal c; connectedness then forces this to be the sole edge and n=c. The first split may preserve Phi. Since n>=3, the remaining edge has weight n-1>=2; split its opposite endpoint, whose size is still n>n-1, to obtain a strict improvement. Therefore any weighted-overlap pair is strictly below the maximum for n>=3.","F1/degree-floor: For integer d>=1, phi(d)>=2(d-1), equality exactly at d=1,2. Equality is direct at 1,2. For real d>=2, the derivative of phi(d)-2(d-1) is log2(d)+1/ln(2)-2>0.","F1/simple-graph-upper-equality: At discrete join every edge weight is one. The connected simple bipartite graph has n edges, v<=n+1 vertices and sum_degrees=2n. Thus Phi=sum phi(d)>=2(2n-v)>=2(n-1), giving RI<=log2(n)-2+2/n=c_n. Equality requires v=n+1 and all degrees in {1,2}: a connected tree with maximum degree two is a path. Conversely a path has two degree-one vertices and n-1 degree-two vertices and attains c_n.","F1/trivial-meet-upper-equality: A trivial-meet weighted pair can be split to a simple connected pair without decreasing RI; the latter is bounded by c_n. For n>=3 strict-overlap excludes weighted pairs from equality, leaving exactly simple paths, including their atom relabellings and interchange of X/Y.","F1/component-decomposition: For a nontrivial meet, its blocks are the connected components C_t of the block graph, with sizes n_t. X, Y and join refine meet, so entropy chain rules give H(X)=H(meet)+sum_t(n_t/n)H(X|C_t), and likewise for Y and join. Consequently RI=sum_t(n_t/n)RI_t, each restricted pair having trivial meet.","F1/component-upper-equality: Every RI_t<=c_(n_t). Here c_1=c_2=0; for real x>=2, c_prime(x)=1/(x ln(2))-2/x^2>0. Therefore c_k<c_n for every integer 1<=k<n when n>=3. With more than one component, all n_t<n, and RI<=sum_t(n_t/n)c_(n_t)<c_n. Thus equality for n>=3 forces trivial meet and the path characterization above.","F1/small-boundary: For n=1 the unique partition pair gives RI=0=c_1. For n=2 the two partitions form a chain; for any pair its meet and join are the pair itself in some order, so RI=0=c_2. Hence every pair attains the zero maximum in these two cases."]}
{"id":"theory/source/F2","statement":"On twelve discrete points, five-point arcs starting at 0, 4 and 8 meet in every pair and not jointly. This finite example does not prove the absence of every finite Helly bound.","status":"proven","references":["vectors/laws/t38/circle12.json","test/t38-forms.test.ts"],"proof":["The explicitly listed finite arcs have pair intersections {4}, {0}, {8}; their triple intersection is empty."]}
{"id":"theory/source/F3","statement":"The four seller/buyer/carrier/auditor halfplanes conflict with seller/auditor witness; x>=0,y>=0,x+y<=-1 is pairwise consistent and jointly empty. Fixed nonnegative certificates verify these finite cases only.","status":"proven","references":["vectors/laws/t38/halfplanes.json","test/t38-forms.test.ts"],"proof":["Nonnegative weights (1,0,0,1) combine the four inequalities into 0<=-2, certifying the seller/auditor conflict.","Weights (1,1,1) combine x>=0, y>=0, x+y<=-1 into 0<=-1. The supplied witness for each pair proves that this three-member conflict is minimal."]}
{"id":"theory/source/F4","statement":"The handed expression Q(n,p,s)=log2(n/p)-h(s/p) has proved integer-domain extrema under p|n and 0<=s<p. Its identification with Shannon information or a physical worst-case phase objective is not established: n=p=2,s=1 gives Q=-1.","status":"open","references":[]}
{"id":"theory/source/F5","statement":"The defined single-covering-channel bound is proved under its declared conditions. Duplicated/disjoint/triangle partial-factor governance is not specified: joint activation/noise and the selection or combination of conflicting active outputs are missing. Existing M/3 rules are not silently transferred to M/4.","status":"open","references":[]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/review/H3","statement":"experiment: one-origin finite structural observation; physical interpretation open.","status":"open","references":["src/laws/laws.bound"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/review/H5","statement":"experiment: finite structural interpretation inherits H3 experiment core; general physical interpretation remains open.","status":"open","references":["src/laws/laws.bound"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/review/offers/field/decohere","statement":"decohere retains its finite calculation; the general physical interpretation inherited from H5 remains open.","status":"open","references":["src/views/field/physics.ts","test/statements-consumers-042.test.ts"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/review/offers/field/distinct","statement":"distinct retains its finite calculation; the general physical interpretation inherited from H5 remains open.","status":"open","references":["src/views/field/physics.ts","test/statements-consumers-042.test.ts"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/review/offers/field/sameObject","statement":"sameObject retains its finite calculation; the general physical interpretation inherited from H5 remains open.","status":"open","references":["src/views/field/physics.ts","test/statements-consumers-042.test.ts"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/review/offers/field/budget","statement":"budget retains its finite calculation; the general physical interpretation inherited from H5 remains open.","status":"open","references":["src/views/field/physics.ts","test/statements-consumers-042.test.ts"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/review/T40-T44","statement":"experiment: declared graph/placement samples remain usable in their finite scope. T18 union bound supplies no currency, graph geometry, transferability or valuation theorem. T41 and downstream declarations inherit this unresolved experimental interpretation.","status":"open","references":["src/laws/laws.bound"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/review/T87-bounded","statement":"Principal angular separation fixes periodicity of the unit-amplitude identity; the historical raw-angle formula is retained in the evidence fixture. Laminarity and T88/T89 policy/ratio generalizations remain open. F4 domain and objective remain open.","status":"open","references":["src/laws/laws.bound"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/review/open-regions","statement":"Unspecified tolerant intersection, partial-factor governance, the physical interpretation/objective of F4, and generalized dissent-only partial-cause identification remain named open. The mathematical F1 proof and corrected law boundaries have their own proof entries.","status":"open","references":["src/laws/laws.bound"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/research-label/T40-cost-by-density","statement":"Documentary research label: experiment-only finite/structural evidence; general/physical interpretation open. The general interpretation remains unproved.","status":"experiment","references":["src/laws/laws.bound"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/research-label/T44-an-origin-is-worth-its-reach","statement":"Documentary research label: experiment-only finite/structural evidence; general/physical interpretation open. The general interpretation remains unproved.","status":"experiment","references":["src/laws/laws.bound"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/research-label/H3-a-distant-signature-moves-a-cell-that-did-not-move","statement":"Documentary research label: experiment-only finite/structural evidence; general/physical interpretation open. The general interpretation remains unproved.","status":"experiment","references":["src/laws/laws.bound"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/research-label/H5-hilbert-is-the-obligatory-without-four-fields","statement":"Documentary research label: experiment-only finite/structural evidence; general/physical interpretation open. The general interpretation remains unproved.","status":"experiment","references":["src/laws/laws.bound"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/T12","statement":"For uniform truth on n>=2 symbols and an independent covering-cause indicator of probability c, the active cause copies one symmetric channel of accuracy q. Revealing the indicator bounds information by log2(n)-c*[h(q)+(1-q)*log2(n-1)]. This is an upper bound, not finite-sample information or an achievability claim. Partial-factor governance remains open. Returned bits follow the explicit binary64 approximation policy, without directed-rounding certification.","status":"proven","references":["src/measures/capacity.ts","test/measures/t12-capacity.test.ts","vectors/measures/t12/expected.json"],"proof":["For truth T and independent covering indicator C, I(T;Y) <= I(T;Y,C) = (1-c) I(T;Y|C=0) + c I(T;Y|C=1). The first conditional information is at most log2(n); the copied symmetric active symbol has conditional entropy h(q)+(1-q)log2(n-1), uniform marginal and information log2(n) minus that entropy. Combining terms gives the T12 bound; a deterministic injective replication of Y preserves its information. No achievability or private-channel model follows."]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/T18","statement":"For events in one probability space with justified upper bounds p_i on their failure probabilities, simultaneous success has probability at least max(0,1-sum(p_i)); independence is unnecessary. Declared references do not establish the probability hypotheses. Tolerant intersection remains undefined.","status":"proven","references":["src/measures/confidence.ts","test/measures/confidence.test.ts","vectors/measures/confidence/expected.json"],"proof":["For failure events F_i, 1_(union F_i)<=sum_i 1_(F_i) pointwise.","Expectation gives P(union F_i)<=sum_i P(F_i)<=sum_i p_i. Complement and nonnegativity give max(0,1-sum_i p_i).","The empty family yields success probability 1; sum_i p_i>=1 yields only the trivial lower bound 0. No independence premise is used."]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/A7-T90/narrowing","statement":"For fixed coordinates, resolution and context, a narrowing of finite admissible state sets cannot increase their log-cardinality. When the nonempty set is uniformly weighted, log-cardinality equals Shannon entropy. Withdrawals and witnessed local joins can increase freedom at the same existing coordinate. Empty sets retain the existing zero-debit convention; the entropy identity applies only to nonempty uniform sets.","status":"proven","proof":["A narrowing gives S_new subset S_old, hence |S_new|<=|S_old|; log2 is increasing on positive cardinalities.","If a narrowing empties the set, the existing zero-debit convention gives 0, no larger than the nonnegative debit before narrowing.","Uniform weights 1/m give -sum_(j=1..m) (1/m)log2(1/m)=log2(m).","The supplied same-object join and exact-withdrawal witnesses disprove the broader exclusivity claims."],"references":["test/laws-boundaries-042.test.ts","vectors/laws/a7-t90.json"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/A9-T99/finite-evaluation","statement":"Finite records over a finite world with a valid acyclic dependency order and total lattice operations admit finite ancestry evaluation. This conclusion concerns the existing traversal, not iteration of an arbitrary monotone map.","status":"proven","proof":["restOf expands each distinct reachable name at most once because held suppresses duplicate expansion. Finite lists and a finite world bound the total expansions and appended edges.","parts evaluates finite live marks and claims using the supplied total lattice operations; meet adds one finite operation."],"references":["src/lattice/obligatory.ts","src/field/meet.ts","test/fold-boundaries-042.test.ts","vectors/laws/a9-t99.json"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/A9-T99/iteration","statement":"Monotonicity on a finite lattice does not guarantee stabilization from every seed: interval reflection alternates two incomparable singleton elements. Additional operator, seed and progress/height hypotheses are needed for an iterative-convergence theorem. For a fixed monotone map on a finite poset, a seed comparable to its first iterate generates an ascending or descending chain that stabilizes; arbitrary seeds remain outside that conclusion.","status":"open","references":["test/fold-boundaries-042.test.ts","vectors/laws/a9-t99.json"],"proof":["If x0<=f(x0), monotonicity inductively gives x_k<=x_(k+1); the reversed inequality gives a descending chain.","A finite poset admits only finitely many strict changes along a chain; equality x_k=x_(k+1) is a fixed point of the same map."]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/T99/reachable-cycle-refusal","statement":"For finite dependency records, restOf rejects every directed cycle reachable from the supplied queried cell, including cycles confined to other ancestors. Unreachable cycles do not affect the query; shared acyclic ancestors and missing references retain their previous behavior.","status":"proven","references":["src/lattice/obligatory.ts","test/fold-boundaries-042.test.ts","vectors/laws/a9-t99.json"],"proof":["Breadth-first expansion records all and only reachable names and their outgoing dependencies; reaching the queried name already rejects its self-return.","The iterative depth-first visiting set is exactly the current active path. An edge to a visiting name closes a directed cycle; completed names do not create false cycles at shared ancestors.","Every reachable directed cycle yields such a back edge in a complete depth-first traversal; finite adjacency and one expansion per name ensure termination."]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/F1/simple-connected-block-graphs","statement":"For uniformly weighted partitions with trivial meet and discrete join, RI<=log2(n)-2+2/n. In this restricted class equality requires a path block graph. The unrestricted reductions and equality transfer remain open.","status":"proven","proof":["Trivial meet and discrete join give a connected simple bipartite block graph with n edges. Its vertex degrees are positive integers with sum 2n and vertex count v<=n+1.","For integer d>=1, phi(d)=d log2(d)>=2(d-1), with equality exactly at d=1,2. For real d>=2 the derivative of phi(d)-2(d-1) is log2(d)+1/ln(2)-2>0; d=1,2 give equality directly.","Under the uniform measure, H(X)=log2(n)-sum_X d log2(d)/n and H(Y)=log2(n)-sum_Y d log2(d)/n. Trivial meet has entropy 0 and discrete join entropy log2(n), so RI=log2(n)-sum_vertices phi(d)/n directly. Thus sum phi(d)>=4n-2v>=2n-2 and RI<=c_n.","Equality requires v=n+1 and every degree in {1,2}: the connected graph is a tree of maximum degree two, hence a path. A path attains c_n since its degrees are two ones and n-1 twos."],"references":["test/f1-boundary-042.test.ts","vectors/laws/f1/path-witness.json","test/vectors-harness.ts"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/L2/finite-convex-Helly","statement":"For finite convex families in R^d, intersection of every subfamily of at most d+1 implies total intersection; d+1 is sharp for d>=1.","status":"proven","proof":["For d+2 points in R^d, affine dependence supplies coefficients alpha_i with sum alpha_i=0 and sum alpha_i*x_i=0. Positive and negative coefficients have equal positive total, yielding a common convex-combination point z (Radon).","For d+2 convex sets, choose x_i in all sets except C_i. For each C_i, one Radon group omits i; all its points belong to C_i, so z belongs to C_i by convexity.","For a larger finite family, replace the final two sets by their intersection. Every subfamily of at most d+1 of the reduced family intersects: those involving the merged set reduce to the d+2 base case. Induction gives the whole intersection.","The d coordinate inequalities x_i>=0 and sum_i x_i<=-1 are jointly inconsistent but every proper subfamily is feasible; thus d+1 is sharp for d>=1."],"references":["test/t38-forms.test.ts","vectors/laws/t38/halfplanes.json"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/F4/provisional-arithmetic","statement":"For fixed positive integers n,p with p dividing n, Q=log2(n/p)-h(s/p) on integer s in [0,p) has maximum log2(n/p) at s=0. For p=1 this is also the minimum; even p>=2 minimizes at p/2 with value log2(n/p)-1; odd p>=3 minimizes at (p-1)/2 and (p+1)/2 with value log2(n/p)-h((p-1)/(2p)).","status":"proven","proof":["Binary entropy is symmetric around 1/2, with h_prime(t)=log2((1-t)/t) and h_second(t)=-1/(ln(2)*t*(1-t))<0 for 0<t<1.","It rises strictly to 1/2 and falls strictly after 1/2. The maximum grid entropy occurs at the nearest integer shifts to p/2; the minimum grid entropy is at the sole included endpoint s=0.","For p=1 the only shift is zero. These are real-expression extrema; binary64 can flatten sufficiently close values and is not an exact extremum certificate."],"references":["test/measures/t87-phase.test.ts","vectors/laws/t87/phase-controls.json"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/tolerant-intersection","statement":"Open definition: neither operator/domain, tolerance units, nor guaranteed event is specified. For singleton candidates {0},{2} and parameter one, allowing one failed claim yields {0,2}, radius-one dilation followed by intersection yields {1}, and ordinary intersection is empty. These different operations cannot be chosen from the supplied parameter alone.","status":"open","references":[]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/partial-factor-governance","statement":"Open definition: the supplied sharing graph and n,c,q do not specify selection/combination when simultaneous overlapping factors produce different outputs, nor their joint activation/noise law. First-output and last-output selection differ on outputs 0 and 1. No generalized M/4 result or implementation follows without these premises.","status":"open","references":["src/measures/capacity.ts"]}
{"id":"obligations/theory/Shannon-Helly","statement":"A Helly-number claim for Shannon partitions needs a declared family of constraints and an inhabited/conflict predicate. The cyclic-arc counterexamples do not prove such a claim for partitions.","status":"open","references":[]}
